INT. ORCHARD HOUSE - KITCHEN - EARLY MORNING

A farm kitchen before sun-up. A pot of oatmeal rests on the stove. A thermos, a lunch pail, and a pair of small rubber boots stand by the door. The window is a blue square.

BENEDICT "BEN" OYELARAN-STAHL, fifties, sits at the table with his hat on, turning a coffee cup a quarter turn at a time. His wife ADELAIDE sits across from him and does not touch her bowl. Their son MARCELLUS, seventeen, stands at the sink with a duffel bag over his shoulder, running the tap. Their daughter PHILIPPA, eleven, sits on the stairs in her nightgown, chin on knees.

The tap runs. Nobody turns it off.

BEN
Your mother packed you the good bread.

MARCELLUS
Thanks, Mom.

ADELAIDE
Mind the crust. It's still warm.

PHILIPPA
Marcy, are you taking the tractor?

MARCELLUS
No, Pip. The bus.

PHILIPPA
Then who's going to drive the tractor?

BEN
I will.

PHILIPPA
You don't like the tractor.

BEN
I like it well enough.

Ben stands, crosses to the window, looks out at the dark orchard. His shoulders do not move. Marcellus finally shuts the tap off. The kitchen goes very still.

ADELAIDE
You've got your ticket?

MARCELLUS
In my coat.

ADELAIDE
Show me.

He pulls it out. She reads it as though it were a letter, then folds it carefully along its crease and hands it back. Her fingers linger on his sleeve.

PHILIPPA
Can I come to the bus stop?

BEN
No.

PHILIPPA
Why not?

BEN
Because there's school.

PHILIPPA
It's Saturday.

BEN
(a beat)
So it is.

Marcellus puts down the duffel. Philippa runs down the stairs and hugs his leg. He ruffles her hair. Ben takes his hat off and holds it by the brim.

BEN (CONT'D)
Marcellus.

MARCELLUS
Yes, Dad.

BEN
The trees will still be here.

MARCELLUS
I know.

BEN
When you come back.

MARCELLUS
I know.

Adelaide stands, takes the lunch pail from the shelf, and fills it, slowly, with bread, a wedge of cheese, an apple. She snaps the lid shut and holds it out. He takes it. For a moment both of them keep a hold of it.

ADELAIDE
Go on, then. You'll miss it.

He nods. At the door he sits on the bench and pulls on his boots, taking much longer than boots require. Ben puts his hat on, takes it off, and puts it on again.

PHILIPPA
Marcy?

MARCELLUS
Yeah, Pip?

PHILIPPA
You forgot your thermos.

She holds it up. He takes it and kisses the top of her head, and then, with his free hand, reaches for Ben's. Ben shakes it once, briskly, as he would with a neighbor, and then pulls him in and holds him a second too long.

Marcellus slings the duffel and goes out into the blue. The screen door bangs. Nobody moves. Then Adelaide ladles oatmeal into Ben's bowl and puts it in front of him.

ADELAIDE
Eat.

BEN
I'm not hungry.

ADELAIDE
Eat anyway.

He sits. He eats.
